$d(A,P) < d(A,Q)$ and $d(C,P) < d(C,Q)$ imply $d(B,P) < d(B,Q)$;
$d(A,P) < d(A,Q)$ and $d(C,P) \leq d(C,Q)$ imply $d(B,P) < d(B,Q)$;
$d(A,P) \le d(A,Q)$ and $d(C,P) \leq d(C,Q)$ imply $d(B,P) \le d(B,Q)$.

NOTE: the answer to this related question shows that 3 is true for the Euclidean metric, but not for the taxicab metric. I am interested in 1 and 2 too.

