There is a two-dimensional container filled with $N$ gas atoms, each with an electric charge $q$ and mass $m$. A magnetic field is gradually applied to this system in the direction perpendicular to the plane of the paper until the magnetic flux density reaches $B$. The final distribution of the system is non-thermodynamic equilibrium.
The questions are:

Why does the application of a magnetic field, an operation analogous to the evolution of the Hamiltonian, lead to non-thermodynamic equilibrium effects?

Are there any alternative methods to reach the same conclusion?


The following:

Consider the Liouville Equation:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\dot{r}\frac{\partial f}{\partial r}+\dot{\theta}\frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta}+\dot{p}_r\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_r}+\dot{p}_{\theta}\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_\theta}=0
\end{equation}
The Hamiltonian is given as:
\begin{equation}
H=\frac{p_r^2}{2m}+\frac{(p_\theta-\frac{1}{2}Bqr^2)^2}{2mr^2}
\end{equation}
thus
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\frac{p_r}{m}\frac{\partial f}{\partial r}+(\frac{p_\theta^2}{mr^3}-\frac{B^2q^2}{4m}r)\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_r}=0
\end{equation}
let $\dot{B}$ seem to be $0$. And thermodynamic equilibrium condition at the beginning.
\begin{equation}
f=Ae^{-\beta(\frac{p_r^2+p_\theta^2/r^2}{2m}+\frac{B^2q^2}{8m}r^2)}
\end{equation}
